 ⁡ ( u , v 0 ) = D − 1 d(u,v_{0})=D-1 , we can apply the hypothesis of induction. When P ∩ Λ ≠ ∅ P\cap\Lambda\neq\varnothing , we argue by induction on l = d P ​ ( u , P ∩ Λ ) + d P ​ ( v , P ∩ Λ ) ≥ 2 . l=d_{P}(u,P\cap\Lambda)+d_{P}(v,P\cap\Lambda)\geq 2. The basic case l = 2 l=2 is simple and the process of induction is exactly as above. ∎ Appendix B Comparison with the cluster expansion approach In [ 39 ] , the local dependence of coefficients (LDC) property is proved using cluster expansions for the hard-core model. We first review the cluster expansion formulation. Then, we discuss its limitations in extending to general 2-spin systems, and explain why the Christoffel-Darboux type identity introduced in the present paper can circumvent the obstacle. B.1 Review of the cluster expansion approach For the hard-core model on a graph G = ( V , E ) G=(V,E) with fugacity λ \lambda , the ratio P G , v ​ ( λ ) = λ ​ Z G ∖ N ⁡ [ v ] ​ ( λ ) Z G ​ ( λ ) P_{G,v}(\lambda)=\frac{\lambda Z_{G\setminus N[v]}(\lambda)}{Z_{G}(\lambda)} (which for positive λ \lambda equals the marginal probability that vertex v v is occupied) admits a series expansion around λ = 0 \lambda=0 derived from the cluster expansion of the multivariate independence polynomial. Concretely, viewing Z G ​ ( λ ) Z_{G}(\lambda) as the specialization of the multivariate polynomial Z G ​ ( 𝐰 ) = ∑ I ⊆ V I ​ indep. ∏ u ∈ I w u Z_{G}(\mathbf{w})=\sum_{I\subseteq V\atop I\text{ indep.}}\prod_{u\in I}w_{u} at w u ≡ λ w_{u}\equiv\lambda , one obtains near λ = 0 \lambda=0 P G , v ​ ( λ ) = ∑ k ≥ 1 1 k ! ​ ∑ v 1 , … , v k ∈ V ϕ ⁡ ( G ⁡ ( v 1 , … , v k ) ) ​ m v ​ ( v 1 , … , v k ) ​ λ k , P_{G,v}(\lambda)=\sum_{k\geq 1}\frac{1}{k!}\sum_{v_{1},\dots,v_{k}\in V}\phi\bigl(G(v_{1},\dots,v_{k})\bigr)\,m_{v}(v_{1},\dots,v_{k})\,\lambda^{k}, (4) where G ⁡ ( v 1 , … , v k ) G(v_{1},\dots,v_{k}) is the cluster induced by the (not necessarily distinct) vertices v 1 , … , v k v_{1},\dots,v_{k} , ϕ ⁡ ( H ) \phi(H) is the Ursell function of a graph H H , and m v ​ ( v 1 , … , v k ) m_{v}(v_{1},\dots,v_{k}) counts the occurrences of v v among v 1 , … , v k v_{1},\dots,v_{k} . Because ϕ ⁡ ( H ) = 0 \phi(H)=0 unless H H is connected, the coefficient of λ k \lambda^{k} depends only on the ( k − 1 ) (k-1) -neighborhood of v v in G G . This immediately yields the LDC property for the hard-core model. For graph homomorphism measures, a similar cluster expansion is obtained by realizing the partition function as the independence polynomial of an auxiliary graph Γ \Gamma whose vertices correspond to connected subgraphs of the original graph. Let G = ( V , E ) G=(V,E) be a graph and let A A be a symmetric q × q q\times q matrix. We consider the partition function with an external field ξ ∈ ℂ V × [ q ] \xi\in\mathbb{C}^{V\times[q]} : Z G ( A , ξ ) = ∑ ϕ : V → [ q ] ∏ v ∈ V ξ v , ϕ ⁡ ( v ) ∏ u ​ v ∈ E A ϕ ⁡ ( u ) , ϕ ⁡ ( v ) . Z_{G}(A,\xi)=\sum_{\phi:V\to[q]}\prod_{v\in V}\xi_{v,\phi(v)}\prod_{uv\in E}A_{\phi(u),\phi(v)}. Fix a boundary condition σ : Λ → [ q ] \sigma:\Lambda\to[q] on some Λ ⊆ V ∖ { v } \Lambda\subseteq V\setminus\{v\} . The goal is to expand the ratio P G , v , i ; A σ ​ ( z ) = Z G , v , i σ v , i ​ ( J + z ⁡ ( A − J ) ) Z G σ ​ ( J + z ⁡ ( A − J ) ) P_{G,v,i;A}^{\sigma}(z)=\frac{Z_{G,v,i}^{\sigma_{v,i}}(J+z(A-J))}{Z_{G}^{\sigma}(J+z(A-J))} around z = 0 z=0 , where J J is the all-ones matrix. Auxiliary graph Γ \Gamma . The vertex set of Γ \Gamma consists of all connected subgraphs H = ( S , F ) H=(S,F) of G G with at least one edge ( | F | ≥ 1 |F|\geq 1 ). Two vertices H 1 = ( S 1 , F 1 ) H_{1}=(S_{1},F_{1}) and H 2 = ( S 2 , F 2 ) H_{2}=(S_{2},F_{2}) are adjacent in Γ \Gamma if and only if S 1 ∩ S 2 ≠ ∅ S_{1}\cap S_{2}\neq\emptyset . Thus an independent set in Γ \Gamma corresponds to a collection of pairwise vertex-disjoint connected subgraphs of G G . Weights. For a vertex H = ( S , F ) H=(S,F) of Γ \Gamma , its weight w σ ​ ( H ) w^{\sigma}(H) is defined as w σ ​ ( H ) := z | F | ​ Z H σ ​ ( A − J , ξ ) ( ∏ v ∈ S ∖ Λ ∑ i = 1 q ξ v , i ) ⋅ ∏ v ∈ Λ ∩ S ξ v , σ ⁡ ( v ) , w^{\sigma}(H):=\frac{z^{|F|}\,Z_{H}^{\sigma}(A-J,\xi)}{\Bigl(\prod_{v\in S\setminus\Lambda}\sum_{i=1}^{q}\xi_{v,i}\Bigr)\cdot\prod_{v\in\Lambda\cap S}\xi_{v,\sigma(v)}}, where Z H σ ​ ( A − J , ξ ) Z_{H}^{\sigma}(A-J,\xi) is the partition function on H H with edge matrix A − J A-J and external field ξ \xi , under the boundary condition σ \sigma restricted to Λ ∩ S \Lambda\cap S . Define also the prefactor p σ ​ ( ξ ) := ( ∏ v ∈ V ∖ Λ ∑ i = 1 q ξ v , i ) ⋅ ∏ v ∈ Λ ξ v , σ ⁡ ( v ) . p^{\sigma}(\xi):=\Bigl(\prod_{v\in V\setminus\Lambda}\sum_{i=1}^{q}\xi_{v,i}\Bigr)\cdot\prod_{v\in\Lambda}\xi_{v,\sigma(v)}. Polymer representation. Lemma 8 of [ 39 ] states that p σ ​ ( ξ ) ​ Z Γ ​ ( w σ ) = Z G σ ​ ( J + z ⁡ ( A − J ) , ξ ) , p^{\sigma}(\xi)\,Z_{\Gamma}(w^{\sigma})=Z_{G}^{\sigma}(J+z(A-J),\xi), where Z Γ ​ ( w σ ) Z_{\Gamma}(w^{\sigma}) is the multivariate independence polynomial of Γ \Gamma evaluated at the weights w σ ​ ( H ) w^{\sigma}(H) : Z Γ ​ ( w σ ) = ∑ ℐ ⊆ V ⁡ ( Γ ) ℐ ​ independent ∏ H ∈ ℐ w σ ​ ( H ) . Z_{\Gamma}(w^{\sigma})=\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}\mathcal{I}\subseteq V(\Gamma)\\ \mathcal{I}\text{ independent}\end{subarray}}\;\prod_{H\in\mathcal{I}}w^{\sigma}(H). Cluster expansion of the ratio. Applying the formal cluster expansion (7) to log ⁡ Z Γ ​ ( w σ ) \log Z_{\Gamma}(w^{\sigma}) and differentiating with respect to ξ v , i \xi_{v,i} , one obtains the following series expansion for the ratio (see [ 39 , Lemma 9] ): P G , v , i ; A σ ​ ( z ) = 1 q + ∑ ℓ ≥ 1 z ℓ ​ ∑ k ≥ 1 1 k ! ​ ∑ ( H 1 , … , H k ) ∈ 𝒞 v , ℓ , k ​ ( G ) ϕ ⁡ ( Γ ⁡ ( H 1 , … , H k ) ) ​ ∂ ∂ ξ v , i ​ ∏ j = 1 k w ^ σ ​ ( H j ) | ξ = 1 , P_{G,v,i;A}^{\sigma}(z)=\frac{1}{q}+\sum_{\ell\geq 1}z^{\ell}\sum_{k\geq 1}\frac{1}{k!}\sum_{(H_{1},\dots,H_{k})\in\mathcal{C}_{v,\ell,k}(G)}\phi\bigl(\Gamma(H_{1},\dots,H_{k})\bigr)\,\frac{\partial}{\partial\xi_{v,i}}\prod_{j=1}^{k}\widehat{w}^{\sigma}(H_{j})\Big|_{\xi=1}, where • 𝒞 v , ℓ , k ​ ( G ) \mathcal{C}_{v,\ell,k}(G) is the collection of sequences ( H 1 , … , H k ) (H_{1},\dots,H_{k}) of connected subgraphs of G G with at least two vertices satisfying ∑ j = 1 k | E ⁡ ( H j ) | = ℓ \sum_{j=1}^{k}|E(H_{j})|=\ell , v ∈ ⋃ j = 1 k V ⁡ ( H j ) v\in\bigcup_{j=1}^{k}V(H_{j}) , and the graph Γ ⁡ ( H 1 , … , H k ) \Gamma(H_{1},\dots,H_{k}) (the subgraph of Γ \Gamma induced by { H 1 , … , H k } \{H_{1},\dots,H_{k}\} ) is connected; • ϕ \phi is the Ursell function; • w ^ σ ​ ( H ) = w σ ​ ( H ) ​ z − | E ⁡ ( H ) | \widehat{w}^{\sigma}(H)=w^{\sigma}(H)\,z^{-|E(H)|} are the scaled weights. The crucial observation is that the ℓ \ell -th coefficient of this series depends only on the subgraphs H j H_{j} whose vertex sets lie within distance ℓ \ell of v v in G G . Consequently, the coefficients of z 0 , z 1 , … , z d − 1 z^{0},z^{1},\dots,z^{d-1} are identical for two boundary conditions that agree on the d d -neighborhood of v v , yielding the LDC property. B.2 Limitations of the cluster expansion approach for general 2‑spin systems Despite its effectiveness for the restricted setting considered in [ 39 ] , the cluster expansion formulation for graph homomorphisms suffers from two fundamental limitations that prevent its application to general 2‑spin systems. 1. Restricted parameter path. The expansion is derived for a partition function whose edge matrix varies along the specific one‑parameter trajectory A ⁡ ( z ) = J + z ⁡ ( A − J ) A(z)=J+z(A-J) . In other words, the zero‑freeness assumption must hold for a whole family of matrices that interpolate linearly between the all‑ones matrix J J and the target matrix A A . For a general 2‑spin system with edge activities β \beta and γ \gamma , the analogous statement would require zero‑freeness for all parameters lying on a line segment connecting ( β , γ ) = ( 1 , 1 ) (\beta,\gamma)=(1,1) to the desired values. This is far more restrictive than the univariate zero‑free regions (e.g., in λ \lambda alone or in β \beta alone). 2. Absence of expansion in the external field variables. The cluster expansion used above treats the auxiliary vertex weights w σ ​ ( H ) w^{\sigma}(H) as power series in the single variable z z , while the external field parameters ξ v , i \xi_{v,i} are merely spectators. No expansion is performed in the ξ \xi variables themselves. Consequently, the method does not yield a multivariate series expansion of the ratios as functions of several independent complex parameters. In a general 2‑spin system, one may need to expand simultaneously in λ \lambda , β \beta , and γ \gamma to obtain LDC around different points (e.g., λ = 0 \lambda=0 for arbitrary β , γ \beta,\gamma , or β ​ γ = 1 \beta\gamma=1 for arbitrary λ \lambda ). The cluster expansion approach, being inherently univariate in its current form, cannot yield such a unified multi‑parameter LDC statement. In contrast, the Christoffel–Darboux type identity established in this paper makes no reference to any specific parameter trajectory and does not require a convergent series expansion in any of the variables. It directly factors the algebraic difference of partition functions with different pinned vertices, revealing powers of λ \lambda and β ​ γ − 1 \beta\gamma-1 that depend only on the graph distance. This purely combinatorial identity therefore yields LDC uniformly for all three parameters and applies to zero‑free regions of arbitrary shape, overcoming the restrictions of the cluster expansion framework. References [ASA70] T. Asano (1970) Lee-Yang theorem and the Griffiths inequality for the anisotropic Heisenberg ferromagnet . Physical Review Letters 24 ( 25 ), pp. 1409 . Cited by: §1 . [BD08] A. Bandyopadhyay and G. David (2008) Counting without sampling: asymptotics of the log‐partition function for certain statistical physics models . Random Structures and Algorithms 33.4 , pp. 452–479 . Cited by: §1 . [BAR82] F. Barahona (1982) On the computational complexity of Ising spin